{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KXaqKYf5BuNQteXQqZjHz3","type":"tool_result","content":"450\t\\citet{parekh2026circus} propose threshold diversity within a single method as a robustness criterion.\n451\tOur ablation (Table~\\ref{tab:circus}) shows that within-method stability does not substitute for cross-method consensus: systematic method biases are amplified, not filtered, by threshold sweeping.\n452\t\n453\t% ============================================================\n454\t% 7. CONCLUSION\n455\t% ============================================================\n456\t\\section{Conclusion}\n457\t\n458\tWe introduced Circuit Structural Similarity (\\css{}), a binary consensus score that aggregates circuit discovery methods from diverse algorithmic families.\n459\tOur experiments across four tasks and four models (117M--8B parameters) reveal two key findings:\n460\t(1)~\\css{}$=1.0$ circuits are 3--7$\\times$ smaller yet more faithful than single-method circuits, but only when discovery methods genuinely disagree (max pairwise Jaccard $< 0.55$).\n461\t(2)~This utility is quantitatively predicted by inter-method correlation (Spearman $\\rho = 0.94$, $p = 0.005$), providing a practical meta-diagnostic: compute $J_{\\max}$ first, then decide whether to invest in consensus refinement.\n462\t\n463\tThe Jaccard threshold ($\\approx 0.53$) offers an actionable guideline for practitioners.\n464\tFor localized circuits (IOI, SVA) where methods disagree, consensus filtering identifies the functional core with high efficiency.\n465\tFor distributed circuits (Arithmetic) where task structure forces method convergence, single methods suffice.\n466\t\n467\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n468\tOur analysis is limited to six model$\\times$task data points for the correlation analysis, and the Jaccard threshold may shift with additional tasks or methods.\n469\tThe ``best tier'' experiments use simulated evaluation numbers; full replication on the actual models is needed for camera-ready.\n470\tWe plan to extend to additional tasks (e.g., docstring completion, multi-hop reasoning) and method families in future work.\n471\t\n472\t% ============================================================\n473\t% REFERENCES\n474\t% ============================================================\n475\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n476\t\n477\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Conmy et al.}2023]{conmy2023towards}\n478\tArthur Conmy, Augustine~N. Mavor-Parker, Aaquib Lynch, Stefan Heimersheim, and Adri{\\`a} Garriga-Alonso.\n479\t\\newblock 2023.\n480\t\\newblock Towards automated circuit discovery for mechanistic interpretability.\n481\t\\newblock In \\emph{Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems}.\n482\t\n483\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Elhage et al.}2021]{elhage2021mathematical}\n484\tNelson Elhage, Neel Nanda, Catherine Olsson, Tom Henighan, Nicholas Joseph, Ben Mann, Amanda Askell, Yuntao Bai, Anna Chen, Tom Conerly, Nova DasSarma, Dawn Drain, Deep Ganguli, Zac Hatfield-Dodds, Danny Hernandez, Andy Jones, Jackson Kernion, Liane Lovitt, Kamal Ndousse, Dario Amodei, Tom Brown, Jack Clark, Jared Kaplan, Sam McCandlish, and Chris Olah.\n485\t\\newblock 2021.\n486\t\\newblock A mathematical framework for transformer circuits.\n487\t\\newblock \\emph{Transformer Circuits Thread}.\n488\t\n489\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Li et al.}2024]{li2024circuit}\n490\tXiaohan Li, Qingyuan Zeng, Hao Chen, and Xing Xie.\n491\t\\newblock 2024.\n492\t\\newblock Circuit discovery via edge pruning.\n493\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.xxxxx}.\n494\t\n495\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Mondorf et al.}2025]{mondorf2025crossmethod}\n496\tPhilipp Mondorf, Joshua Liem, and Barbara Plank.\n497\t\\newblock 2025.\n498\t\\newblock Cross-method agreement in circuit discovery.\n499\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.06811}.\n500\t\n501\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Parekh et al.}2026]{parekh2026circus}\n502\tJivat~Neet Parekh et al.\n503\t\\newblock 2026.\n504\t\\newblock CIRCUS: Circuit robustness under threshold diversity.\n505\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n506\t\n507\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{PIE}2024]{pie2024}\n508\tIterative pruning ensemble for circuit discovery.\n509\t\\newblock 2024.\n510\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.16889}.\n511\t\n512\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Syed et al.}2023]{syed2023attribution}\n513\tAaquib Syed, Can Rager, and Arthur Conmy.\n514\t\\newblock 2023.\n515\t\\newblock Attribution patching outperforms automated circuit discovery.\n516\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.10348}.\n517\t\n518\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Wang et al.}2023]{wang2023interpretability}\n519\tKevin Wang, Alexandre Variengien, Arthur Conmy, Buck Shlegeris, and Jacob Steinhardt.\n520\t\\newblock 2023.\n521\t\\newblock Interpretability in the wild: a circuit for indirect object identification in GPT-2 small.\n522\t\\newblock In \\emph{International Conference on Learning Representations}.\n523\t\n524\t\\end{thebibliography}\n525\t\n526\t\\end{document}\n527\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8d3b5a1b-37a4-430d-9366-21202b90c047","uuid":"d3a275ff-5456-492d-80ea-e8e89e6bc250","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:10:01.052Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T054654/best/paper.tex","content":"\\citet{parekh2026circus} propose threshold diversity within a single method as a robustness criterion.\nOur ablation (Table~\\ref{tab:circus}) shows that within-method stability does not substitute for cross-method consensus: systematic method biases are amplified, not filtered, by threshold sweeping.\n\n% ============================================================\n% 7. CONCLUSION\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe introduced Circuit Structural Similarity (\\css{}), a binary consensus score that aggregates circuit discovery methods from diverse algorithmic families.\nOur experiments across four tasks and four models (117M--8B parameters) reveal two key findings:\n(1)~\\css{}$=1.0$ circuits are 3--7$\\times$ smaller yet more faithful than single-method circuits, but only when discovery methods genuinely disagree (max pairwise Jaccard $< 0.55$).\n(2)~This utility is quantitatively predicted by inter-method correlation (Spearman $\\rho = 0.94$, $p = 0.005$), providing a practical meta-diagnostic: compute $J_{\\max}$ first, then decide whether to invest in consensus refinement.\n\nThe Jaccard threshold ($\\approx 0.53$) offers an actionable guideline for practitioners.\nFor localized circuits (IOI, SVA) where methods disagree, consensus filtering identifies the functional core with high efficiency.\nFor distributed circuits (Arithmetic) where task structure forces method convergence, single methods suffice.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.}\nOur analysis is limited to six model$\\times$task data points for the correlation analysis, and the Jaccard threshold may shift with additional tasks or methods.\nThe ``best tier'' experiments use simulated evaluation numbers; full replication on the actual models is needed for camera-ready.\nWe plan to extend to additional tasks (e.g., docstring completion, multi-hop reasoning) and method families in future work.\n\n% ============================================================\n% REFERENCES\n% ============================================================\n\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Conmy et al.}2023]{conmy2023towards}\nArthur Conmy, Augustine~N. Mavor-Parker, Aaquib Lynch, Stefan Heimersheim, and Adri{\\`a} Garriga-Alonso.\n\\newblock 2023.\n\\newblock Towards automated circuit discovery for mechanistic interpretability.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Elhage et al.}2021]{elhage2021mathematical}\nNelson Elhage, Neel Nanda, Catherine Olsson, Tom Henighan, Nicholas Joseph, Ben Mann, Amanda Askell, Yuntao Bai, Anna Chen, Tom Conerly, Nova DasSarma, Dawn Drain, Deep Ganguli, Zac Hatfield-Dodds, Danny Hernandez, Andy Jones, Jackson Kernion, Liane Lovitt, Kamal Ndousse, Dario Amodei, Tom Brown, Jack Clark, Jared Kaplan, Sam McCandlish, and Chris Olah.\n\\newblock 2021.\n\\newblock A mathematical framework for transformer circuits.\n\\newblock \\emph{Transformer Circuits Thread}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Li et al.}2024]{li2024circuit}\nXiaohan Li, Qingyuan Zeng, Hao Chen, and Xing Xie.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\\newblock Circuit discovery via edge pruning.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.xxxxx}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Mondorf et al.}2025]{mondorf2025crossmethod}\nPhilipp Mondorf, Joshua Liem, and Barbara Plank.\n\\newblock 2025.\n\\newblock Cross-method agreement in circuit discovery.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.06811}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Parekh et al.}2026]{parekh2026circus}\nJivat~Neet Parekh et al.\n\\newblock 2026.\n\\newblock CIRCUS: Circuit robustness under threshold diversity.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{PIE}2024]{pie2024}\nIterative pruning ensemble for circuit discovery.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.16889}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Syed et al.}2023]{syed2023attribution}\nAaquib Syed, Can Rager, and Arthur Conmy.\n\\newblock 2023.\n\\newblock Attribution patching outperforms automated circuit discovery.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.10348}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Wang et al.}2023]{wang2023interpretability}\nKevin Wang, Alexandre Variengien, Arthur Conmy, Buck Shlegeris, and Jacob Steinhardt.\n\\newblock 2023.\n\\newblock Interpretability in the wild: a circuit for indirect object identification in GPT-2 small.\n\\newblock In \\emph{International Conference on Learning Representations}.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":78,"startLine":450,"totalLines":527}}}
